# CAPABILITIES — Tokens and trust

File: guide/Safety.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://openooda.org/guide/#Safety.oot

[Home](Home.oot) [Start](Use.oot) [Language](Language.oot) [Commands](Options.oot) [Packages](Packages.oot) [How it works](Limits.oot)

## 1. What a token is

A capability token is a permission you hold as an argument.
Effects need that argument.
You write it as a type: `&FsReadCap`, `&ProcessCap`, `&SysCap`.
The function that does the effect takes the token.
You pass the token in. You do not hide the effect.

Missing, forged, or zero tokens fail closed.
The call does not run.

`main` lists the tokens this program holds.
Helpers take the tokens they need as parameters.
You pass the token down.
You cannot mint a token from bytes.
A forged parent seal does not verify.
HMAC attenuation makes a narrower child seal. The child is not the parent.

No token, no effect. A function cannot mint a token from bytes.

## 2. How you pass one

```
pub fn load_notes(fs: &FsReadCap, path: String) -> Result[String, String] {
    return read_file(fs, path);
}

pub fn main(fs: &FsReadCap) {
    let text = load_notes(fs, "notes.txt");
}
```

`read_file` takes `fs`.
`load_notes` takes `fs`.
`main` takes `fs`.
Every call site shows the permission.

A function that only computes takes no token.

## 3. Prefer the small token

Take the smallest token that covers the effect.
Do not take `&SysCap` when `&FsReadCap` is enough.

| Token | What you use it for |
| :--- | :--- |
| `&FsReadCap` | `read_file` |
| `&FsWriteCap` | `write_file` (needs `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`) |
| `&ProcessCap` | `spawn`, `wait`, `kill`, `process_exec` |
| `&NetCap` / `&HttpCap` / `&TcpCap` / `&UdpCap` | network ops |
| `&EnvCap` | `env_get` for `OODA_*` / `OO_*` keys |
| `&AllocCap` | heap and bump arenas |
| `&ThreadCap` | threads, channels, actor restart |
| `&SysCap` | Landlock jail and `setrlimit` |

Flags and env that go with these tokens live on [Commands](Options.oot).

## 4. Files

Read a file with `&FsReadCap`.

```
pub fn main(fs: &FsReadCap) {
    let body = read_file(fs, "notes.txt");
}
```

Write a file with `&FsWriteCap`.
`write_file` also needs `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
That env is the directory the write may touch.
Unset denies the write.

```
export OODA_FS_WRITEDIR="$PWD"
```

```
pub fn save_notes(fw: &FsWriteCap, path: String, body: String) -> Result[Void, String] {
    return write_file(fw, path, body);
}
```

`ooda fix` writes the same way. See [Start](Use.oot).

## 5. Process, net, and env

`&ProcessCap` is the token for child processes: `spawn`, `wait`, `kill`, `process_exec`.

```
pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap) {
    let out = process_exec(p, "ooda version");
}
```

Network ops take `&HttpCap`, `&TcpCap`, `&UdpCap`, or the broader `&NetCap`.
Prefer the small one that matches the call.

`&EnvCap` lets `env_get` read `OODA_*` and `OO_*` keys.
Host keys stay out.

```
pub fn main(env: &EnvCap) {
    let dir = env_get(env, "OODA_FS_WRITEDIR");
}
```

## 6. Heap and threads

`&AllocCap` is the token for heap and bump arenas.
Take it when you allocate a region.
You do not take it for ordinary locals.

`&ThreadCap` is the token for threads, channels, and actor restart.
Take it when you start work on another thread.
A single-thread program does not need it.

## 7. Sys, Landlock, and quotas

`&SysCap` is the broad OS token.
Use it when you mean the jail or the resource limit.
Do not use it as a shortcut for a file read.

Landlock is a real Linux jail under `&SysCap`.
You pass the directories the process may read and write.

```
import "std/sec/landlock.oo";

pub fn main(sys: &SysCap) {
    let _ = landlock_restrict(sys, "/home/you/work", "/home/you/work");
}
```

Empty allowlists fail closed.
A call with no directories does not open the world.

`setrlimit` also sits under `&SysCap`.
Zero quotas fail closed.
A memory, file, or CPU quota of zero is not a grant.

## 8. Secrets

Mark a secret field. The compiler tracks it.

```
type Creds = struct { token: String, name: String };
type User = struct { creds: Creds };
// SECRET: token

pub fn show(user: User) {
    println(user.creds.name);
}
```

`println` of a secret field is refused.
The sibling non-secret field is allowed.
`println(user.creds.token)` fails.
`println(user.creds.name)` runs.

`process_exec` is a secret sink.
Do not pass a secret field into it.

## 9. Packages

A lock line carries a sha256 and a declared cap name.

```
name@version#sha256#CapName
```

The hash is the bytes.
The cap name is the token that package may use.

Ingest pins and wraps.
The pin is the sha256.
The wrap is how the blob is stored.

`ooda add hello` verifies the stock catalog with minisign.

Path traversal, extra caps, and yank refuse.

`ooda launch --exec` runs the gated payload after sha, caps, and yank are ok.

How to add a library: [Packages](Packages.oot).

## 10. Binary pins

The lock pins the exact bytes of a package with a sha256 hash.
A foreign ELF file does not receive Ooda tokens from a lock line.
Measured isolate hashes the payload, the token, and the domain.
That is a hash, not a hardware enclave.
The file on disk stays the same so a rebuild can match.
Load-time layout change is research, not this path.

## 11. How to use caps well

1. Declare the tokens the function actually uses.
2. Pass them down. Every effect call takes its token.
3. Prefer the small token.
4. Set `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` before you write.
5. Mark secret fields. Keep them out of `println` and `process_exec`.
6. Read the declared cap on the lock line before you launch.
7. Treat the lock hash as the identity of the bytes.

The product path still typechecks, emits C, compiles with host `gcc`, and runs.
See [How it works](Limits.oot).

This page: https://openooda.org/guide/#Safety.oot
